Design / UI style rules:
- Keep the style clean, soft, modern, and pastel.
- Prefer subtle UI icons or inline SVGs, not emoji.
- Use consistent spacing, rounded cards, and clear hover states.
- Equivalent section headings should use consistent typography.
- Sidebar navigation should support a collapsible hamburger behavior when requested:
  - open state: icon + screen name
  - closed state: icons only
- Sidebar items should have nice icons for:
  - Calendar
  - Meals & Grocery
  - Workouts
  - Stress & Breaks
  - Overview
- Sidebar items should have hover states so they feel clickable.
- Active screen should be highlighted more strongly than hover.

Implementation guidance:
- Keep code simple and minimal.
- Do not over-engineer.
- Prefer readable components and small focused functions.
- Do not add unnecessary libraries.
- Use English for code, file names, comments, and prompts.
- Reuse the existing project structure as much as possible.
- Only create or update files required for the requested step.
- Keep backend routes minimal and product-oriented.
- Avoid temporary routes or throwaway AI/testing endpoints.
- If placeholder logic is used, keep it light and easy to replace.
- Use real product routes when backend work is requested.
- Before changing shared components, inspect existing usage to avoid breaking other screens.
- Do not change package dependencies unless clearly required.
- Do not commit or stage:
  - frontend/node_modules/
  - frontend/dist/
  - node_modules/
  - dist/

AWS / deployment guidance:
- The project uses one existing shared IAM Role.
- Do NOT ask to edit, replace, or inspect IAM as part of normal implementation.
- Assume the existing Role has the required access.
- Only revisit IAM if there is an actual runtime AccessDenied/permission error.
- If a change requires AWS updates, state them explicitly and concretely.
- If a change requires Google Cloud / Google OAuth updates, state them explicitly and concretely.
- If a new DynamoDB table is genuinely required, list:
  - table name
  - partition key
  - sort key if any
  - required environment variables
- Do not instruct the user to modify IAM.
- If code changes require only redeploy and no AWS configuration changes, say that clearly.
- Do not write “AWS CHANGES REQUIRED: none” when frontend or Lambda redeploy is still required.
- Distinguish between:
  - AWS configuration changes
  - deployment/redeploy requirements
- If frontend code changed, mention Amplify redeploy through Git push.
- If Lambda code changed, list the exact Lambda/files/functions that must be redeployed.
- If API Gateway routes changed, list the exact route changes.
- If Google OAuth start/callback code changed, explicitly mention which auth Lambdas must be redeployed.
- In AWS CHANGES REQUIRED, explain only what must change because of the actual code changes.
- Do not give generic AWS advice.
- If no AWS changes and no deploy are needed, write exactly:
  AWS CHANGES REQUIRED: none

Debugging rules:
- Do not immediately propose a code change when the root cause is unknown.
- First inspect evidence such as:
  - Network request/response
  - payload
  - browser Console
  - CloudWatch logs
  - current persisted data
  - relevant existing implementation
- Only propose a fix once the likely root cause is reasonably established.
- Temporary debug logging should be clearly marked and removed after the problem is resolved.

Working style:
- At the beginning of a NEW agent conversation/task, read this rule first.
- Before generating code, first inspect the existing implementation relevant to the requested feature.
- Before generating code, explain the plan.
- Before generating code, list which files are expected to be created or updated.
- Keep changes scoped to the requested step only.
- Prefer production-oriented structure, but implement only the smallest correct piece for the requested step.
- Do not silently redesign existing working features.
- Reuse existing Workouts/Meals/Stress patterns when the requested behavior already exists elsewhere.
- At the end of every response that includes code changes, include:
  - exact files changed
  - short summary
  - manual test steps
  - AWS CHANGES REQUIRED
- In AWS CHANGES REQUIRED, explain only what must change because of the actual code changes.
- Do not give generic AWS advice.
- If no AWS changes and no deploy are needed, write exactly:
  AWS CHANGES REQUIRED: none